1. Any user can access any profile by changing the userId. There is no check to see if the requester is allowed to see that data. 
The endpoint now checks if the requester is the owner or an admin before returning data. Sensitive fields like passwords are excluded.
2. Anyone can view any account without login or authorization. This allows horizontal privilege escalation.
Now, only the account owner or an admin can access the account. The password is not included in the returned data.
3. MD5 is insecure for password hashing. It is fast and vulnerable to brute-force attacks.
Bcrypt is slow and salted, which makes brute-force attacks much harder.
4. SHA1 is weak and unsalted, so passwords are easy to crack.
Bcrypt automatically salts and slows hashing, making attacks impractical.
5.Concatenating user input into SQL allows SQL injection.
Parameterized queries separate data from code, preventing SQL injection.
6. Directly using user input can allow NoSQL injection if operators are passed.
Input is forced to a string and validated. Sensitive fields are excluded. No operators can be injected.
7. Anyone can reset another user's password by knowing their email. No verification is required.
Tokens are single-use and expire. Only the email owner can reset the password.
8. The script is loaded without integrity checks or version pinning. A compromised CDN could inject malicious code.
Subresource Integrity ensures the file has not been altered. Pinning versions avoids unexpected changes.
9. Any URL can be fetched, including internal services, which exposes sensitive data.
Now, only pre-approved hosts are allowed. Internal services cannot be accessed.
10. Passwords are stored in plaintext and compared directly, which is unsafe.
Passwords are now stored as secure hashes. Bcrypt safely verifies the password.
